3.3 CUDA memory allocation and movement
Allocation and movement are part of the performance model, not background bookkeeping. A program can be mathematically correct and still slow or unstable because it allocates unpredictably, fragments memory, or synchronizes copies.

3.3.1 Host-device and peer-transfer ownership
Explicit transfers are initiated by framework placement calls or CUDA runtime APIs and executed over the path available between the source and destination. The list identifies the objects and hardware conditions that govern those transfers.

Pinned host memory: Page-locked CPU memory that allows more efficient host-to-device and device-to-host transfer. It is commonly used by DataLoaders and async copies, but pinning too much host memory can hurt the operating system.
Asynchronous copy and streams: A copy can overlap with compute only when the copy is issued asynchronously, the host memory path supports it, and stream dependencies do not serialize the work. A later synchronization can still expose the full cost.
cudaMemcpy: The CUDA runtime copy operation used to move bytes between host and device or between devices. In PyTorch this is usually hidden behind .to(device), .cuda(), .cpu(), or tensor copy calls.
Direct Memory Access (DMA): A hardware transfer path that can move data without the CPU copying each byte. Pinned host memory helps DMA-based GPU transfers, but the transfer still consumes interconnect bandwidth.
PCIe transfer: The common host/device path. Its bandwidth is far below HBM bandwidth, so hot training and inference loops should avoid repeated CPU/GPU round trips.
Peer transfer: A GPU-to-GPU copy that can avoid host staging when peer access, topology, and the runtime configuration permit a direct path.

A low-level CUDA lifecycle makes the hidden costs visible: allocate device memory, copy inputs from host to device, launch the kernel, synchronize only when required, copy results back if the host needs them, and free or reuse device memory. PyTorch hides most of these calls, but the performance costs remain.
Example: Host-device copy lifecycle. This compact CUDA C++ example makes the host-to-device lifecycle explicit: allocate device storage, submit an asynchronous copy, launch a kernel, copy the result back, synchronize, and release the resources.

Code example: Host-device copy lifecycle

// What this code shows:
// - cudaMallocHost creates page-locked host buffers so the copy engine can use a direct DMA-
//   friendly path.
// - cudaMemcpyAsync submits host-device transfers to the stream; the kernel waits on the same
//   stream order.
// - The kernel reads tensors already resident in GPU HBM; it does not move each value through the
//   CPU.
// - cudaStreamSynchronize makes the host wait for the device before the result is consumed or
//   resources are released.
//
#include &lt;cuda_runtime.h&gt;

__global__ void add(const float* x, const float* y, float* out, int n) {
    int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    if (i &lt; n) out[i] = x[i] + y[i];
}

int main() {
    const int n = 1 &lt;&lt; 20;
    const size_t bytes = n * sizeof(float);
    float *h_x, *h_y, *h_out, *d_x, *d_y, *d_out;
    cudaStream_t stream;

    cudaMallocHost(&h_x, bytes);  // page-locked host buffers
    cudaMallocHost(&h_y, bytes);
    cudaMallocHost(&h_out, bytes);
    cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast&lt;void**&gt;(&d_x), bytes);
    cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast&lt;void**&gt;(&d_y), bytes);
    cudaMalloc(reinterpret_cast&lt;void**&gt;(&d_out), bytes);
    cudaStreamCreate(&stream);

    cudaMemcpyAsync(d_x, h_x, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice, stream);
    cudaMemcpyAsync(d_y, h_y, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice, stream);
    add&lt;&lt;&lt;(n + 255) / 256, 256, 0, stream&gt;&gt;&gt;(d_x, d_y, d_out, n);
    cudaMemcpyAsync(h_out, d_out, bytes, cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost, stream);
    cudaStreamSynchronize(stream);

    cudaStreamDestroy(stream);
    cudaFree(d_x); cudaFree(d_y); cudaFree(d_out);
    cudaFreeHost(h_x); cudaFreeHost(h_y); cudaFreeHost(h_out);
}

What synchronization hides
Many timing mistakes come from measuring a host API call without realizing GPU work is asynchronous. Conversely, an innocent-looking call such as .item(), .cpu(), or an explicit synchronize can force the host to wait for earlier GPU work.

A host-device copy moves bytes between CPU memory and GPU HBM. CUDA exposes the mechanism through operations such as cudaMemcpy and cudaMemcpyAsync; PyTorch usually exposes the same behavior through tensor placement calls such as .to('cuda'), .cuda(), .cpu(), and DataLoader transfer paths.
The CPU submits the transfer request, and DMA-capable copy hardware performs the bulk byte movement. Pinned host memory matters because the pages stay resident while the DMA transfer is in flight; ordinary pageable memory may require an extra staging copy through a pinned buffer.
Pinned host memory is page-locked staging memory for host-device transfers. It prevents the operating system from moving or paging out the host buffer while a DMA transfer is in flight. It improves transfer mechanics, while HBM remains the high-bandwidth device-memory tier and GPU cache residency remains hardware-managed.
Mapped host memory and managed memory are placement mechanisms. Mapped host memory lets a GPU kernel access a host allocation through the system interconnect, which can be useful for small or irregular control data and slow for bulk tensor traffic. Managed memory can migrate or prefetch pages between processors; cudaMemAdvise and cudaMemPrefetchAsync guide residency, while ordinary L1 and L2 cache behavior remains hardware-managed.

Pinned-memory staging: A producer places input batches in page-locked host memory so the GPU copy path can use DMA more efficiently.
Double buffering: The next batch is prepared or copied while the current batch is computing, reducing exposed input-transfer time.
Copy-compute overlap: A copy and a kernel run concurrently only when they are issued asynchronously, memory supports the async path, stream dependencies permit overlap, and hardware resources are available.
Synchronization boundary: A later .item(), .cpu(), print, profiler step, or explicit synchronize can reveal copy or kernel time that looked hidden at submission.

Example: Pinned host batch transfer. This abbreviated training-input path assumes that dataset, model, and loss_fn already exist. It demonstrates pinned staging and a non-blocking transfer request, but it does not prove overlap unless a timeline shows copy and compute running concurrently.

Code example: Pinned host batch transfer

# What this code shows:
# - pin_memory=True asks the DataLoader to allocate page-locked host buffers for batches.
# - non_blocking=True lets PyTorch request an asynchronous copy when the source and stream path
#   support it.
# - The model still waits if the kernel needs input_ids before the copy finishes.
# - Nsight Systems or PyTorch Profiler is needed to confirm whether transfer time is hidden or
#   exposed.
#
from torch.utils.data import DataLoader

loader = DataLoader(dataset, batch_size=32, pin_memory=True)

for batch in loader:
    input_ids = batch["input_ids"].to("cuda", non_blocking=True)
    labels = batch["labels"].to("cuda", non_blocking=True)
    logits = model(input_ids)
    loss = loss_fn(logits, labels)
    loss.backward()

Overlap condition
non_blocking=True requests asynchronous submission. PCIe cost is hidden only when the data source, stream ordering, and hardware path allow useful copy-compute overlap.

An asynchronous copy hides transfer time only when pinned memory, stream order, hardware, and independent compute permit real overlap; non_blocking=True alone is not evidence. Once the bytes arrive, the next question is how allocator reuse, fragmentation, and runtime controls affect the device memory available to later kernels.


3.3.2 Allocator and runtime memory controls
Allocation state determines whether the next tensor or workspace can be placed without an expensive driver request or an out-of-memory failure. Runtime controls can influence reuse and cache policy, while hardware still decides exact replacement and residency.

Device tensor: A tensor whose storage is allocated in GPU memory and whose operations normally execute on that GPU. The Python object is host-side metadata; the tensor data pointer refers to device memory.
Caching allocator: PyTorch’s CUDA allocator reserves blocks from the CUDA driver and reuses them for later tensor allocations, reducing repeated driver allocation calls.
Allocated versus reserved: Allocated memory belongs to live tensors. Reserved memory remains in allocator-managed blocks for reuse, so reserved capacity can exceed the currently live tensor footprint.
Fragmentation: Free capacity divided into blocks that cannot satisfy the next allocation shape. Dynamic sequence lengths, workspaces, and long-running serving admission can make it visible.
L2 persisting access window: A CUDA stream or graph setting that marks a global-memory range for preferred L2 retention within hardware and set-aside limits.

The following controls influence resource preference or retention. They guide supported hardware behavior; ordinary application code still cannot assign a tensor to a chosen cache set or permanently lock arbitrary cache lines.



Table 3.1: Runtime memory controls and their enforceable boundaries.











Runtime control
Owner
Useful influence
Boundary




Shared-memory/L1 preference
CUDA runtime or kernel attribute
Can prefer a supported L1/shared-memory split
Does not guarantee exact capacity or residency


L2 persisting window
CUDA stream or graph kernel-node attribute
Can prefer retention for a global-memory range
Cannot exceed set-aside capacity or pin arbitrary lines


Allocator configuration
PyTorch CUDA allocator
Can change block splitting, reuse, and reclamation behavior
Cannot reduce the workload’s true live tensor footprint






Allocator settings can change block reuse and fragmentation, while cache controls can influence retention, but neither reduces the workload’s true live memory or guarantees residency. The next question is how streams and events order allocations, copies, and kernels without adding unnecessary waits.
